  "metricsHeartRate": "Heart rate",
  "metricsRecoveryIntro": "Measures how far your heart rate drops after exertion. A larger drop over 60–120 s usually indicates better cardiovascular fitness and faster recovery.",
  "tipRmssd": "Root Mean Square of Successive Differences – sensitive to short-term, parasympathetic activity. Higher values usually mean better recovery and lower stress.",
  "tipSdnn": "Standard deviation of NN intervals – overall heart rate variability across the whole measurement. Reflects both short- and long-term influences.",
  "tipPnn50": "Proportion of successive NN intervals differing by more than 50 ms. A higher percentage usually indicates stronger parasympathetic activity.",
  "tipMeanRr": "Mean NN (RR) interval length in milliseconds. Inversely related to resting heart rate (longer RR = lower BPM).",
  "tipSd1": "SD1 (Poincaré) – short-term HRV (beat-to-beat variability). Closely related to RMSSD and parasympathetic tone.",
  "tipSd2": "SD2 (Poincaré) – long-term HRV. Captures slower fluctuations and the overall variability trend.",
  "tipSd2Sd1Ratio": "Ratio of long-term to short-term variability. A higher ratio may indicate dominant slow oscillations or elevated stress.",
  "tipLf": "Low Frequency component (0.04–0.15 Hz) – a mix of sympathetic and parasympathetic activity. Shown as a percentage of (LF+HF).",
  "tipHf": "High Frequency component (0.15–0.40 Hz) – predominantly parasympathetic activity (respiratory sinus arrhythmia). Shown as a percentage of (LF+HF).",
  "tipLfHfRatio": "LF/HF ratio – an indicative measure of sympathetic/parasympathetic balance. Interpret it in the context of recording length and conditions.",
  "tipRespiratoryRate": "Respiratory rate (breaths per minute). Derived from PPG amplitude changes, typically 6–20 breaths/min at rest.",
  "tipBaevsky": "Baevsky stress index – an indicative measure of stress load. Higher values may point to greater strain; interpret with the measurement conditions in mind.",
  "tipGenericHrv": "HRV metric. Interpret it in the context of recording length, artefacts and measurement conditions.",
  "tipHr0": "Average heart rate over the first 10 seconds of recovery. Used as the baseline for assessing the drop.",
  "tipHr60": "Heart rate around the 60th second after exertion. Compared with the initial HR to compute HRR60.",
  "tipHrr60": "Heart Rate Recovery over 60 s: the difference between 0–10 s and 50–60 s. Higher = faster recovery.",
  "tipHrSlope": "Linear slope of the heart rate decline over the first 60 s, in bpm/min. More negative = faster decline.",
  "tipHr120": "Heart rate around the 120th second. Suitable for longer recovery protocols and HRR120.",
  "tipHrr120": "Heart Rate Recovery over 120 s: the difference between 0–10 s and 110–120 s. Higher = better fitness.",
  "tipHrTau": "Estimated time constant of the exponential heart rate decay toward baseline (lower means faster recovery).",
