  "aboutHowPpgTitle": "How does PPG measurement work?",
  "aboutHowPpgBody": "Photoplethysmography (PPG) measures changes in blood volume in the vessels beneath your skin. With every heartbeat more blood passes through your finger, absorbing more light, so the camera frame darkens for a moment. The app computes the average brightness of the area under your finger in each frame and derives your pulse from how that changes over time.",
  "aboutFingerTitle": "How should I place my finger?",
  "aboutFingerBody": "1. Make sure your finger is clean.\n2. Place your finger gently on the camera so that it covers it completely.\n3. Do not press too hard, as this distorts the signal.\n4. The torch turns on by itself during the countdown — keep your finger still until measuring begins.",
  "aboutBeforeStartTitle": "What happens before measuring starts",
  "aboutBeforeStartBody": "During the 3–2–1 countdown the app turns on the torch, waits for the camera's automatic adjustments to settle, and then locks exposure and focus. Without that lock, auto-exposure would compensate for exactly the brightness changes your pulse creates. Only then does recording begin, so its start is not affected by the torch switching on or by filters settling.",
  "aboutProcessingTitle": "How the signal is processed",
  "aboutProcessingBody": "1. Each frame contributes the average of the green channel in the area under your finger; if green is too dark, the red channel is used instead.\n2. An adaptive filter driven by accelerometer data suppresses the effect of hand movement.\n3. A median filter then removes isolated spikes, the slowly changing component (breathing, perfusion) is subtracted, and the result is gently smoothed.\n4. The camera does not deliver frames at perfectly even intervals, so the app works with the real frame rate measured from frame timestamps — in practice around 24 frames per second, not 30.",
  "aboutHeartRateTitle": "How is heart rate calculated?",
  "aboutHeartRateBody": "During measurement the pulse is estimated from the signal spectrum and from autocorrelation over the last few seconds; when the estimate is not reliable enough, the app shows nothing rather than an unfounded number.\nIn the result and in your history the pulse is then computed from detected peaks: the peaks corresponding to individual beats are located, the intervals between them are measured, and the pulse comes out as 60 ÷ the average interval in seconds. Each peak position is interpolated between frames so the intervals are not limited by the frame step.",
  "aboutGreenChannelTitle": "Why the green channel?",
  "aboutGreenChannelBody": "Haemoglobin absorbs green light most strongly, so changes in blood perfusion show up most clearly there. Red light passes through a finger more easily, so the red channel serves as a fallback when green is too dark.",
  "aboutAccuracyTitle": "How accurate is it",
  "aboutAccuracyBody": "In August 2026 we validated the processing against a Bittium Faros 180 ECG Holter and a pulse oximeter across measurements with 13 participants. On stored recordings reprocessed with the same detector the app uses, the pulse matched the ECG with a mean deviation below 2 bpm — comparable to how closely the two reference devices agree with each other.\nAccuracy does, however, depend on signal quality: with movement, weak perfusion or a poorly placed finger the estimate is considerably worse. Every measurement therefore carries a quality rating — do not rely on measurements marked as poor.\nTreat the variability parameters (SDNN, RMSSD) as indicative only; the camera's frame rate is coarse for those. This app is not a medical device and is not intended for diagnosis.",
  "infoTitle": "About us",
  "infoIntro": "Hi there!\nWe are delighted to introduce our app, Tepovka, developed as part of a competition organised by the Faculty of Electrical Engineering and Communication at Brno University of Technology. This project is the result of teamwork combining a passion for biomedical engineering with a drive to bring innovative solutions into everyday life.\n\nOur goal is to build an app that makes measuring heart rate simple, using nothing but a smartphone with a camera and a torch. That feature is only the beginning. In the future we plan to extend the app with more advanced capabilities such as blood oxygen saturation (oximetry), cardiac arrhythmia detection or even blood pressure measurement — should research show the technology to be feasible.\n\nThe app is being developed with the support of BUT, which provides not only the technical facilities but also the expert guidance that lets us turn our ideas into reality.",
  "infoTeamTitle": "Our team",
  "infoTeamBody": "We are biomedical engineering students united by a passion for developing healthcare technology. Our team:\n- Štěpán Zelníček\n- David Vavroušek\n- Jakub Kovář\n- Jakub Kaša\n\nEach of us contributes specific skills – from programming through app design to biological data analysis. Our main goal is to create an app that is both user-friendly and technologically advanced, and that serves the general public.",
  "infoFollowTitle": "Follow and support us",
  "infoFollowBody": "We are excited to share our journey with you, and we would be glad if you supported us and followed our progress. We look forward to exploring new possibilities in digital healthcare together!",
  "infoSupportedBy": "With the support of BUT. Thank you",
